PRIVATE REFLECTION: {The key question is which red-suited Qx hand has the most equity against black AA: QJ, QT, or Q9. Flush equity is essentially identical for all three because AA is black and does not block the red flush suit. Pair/trips/two-pair chances involving Q plus the second card are also nearly identical; the rank of J/T/9 does not matter much against AA once it makes two pair or trips. The real differentiator is straight potential.

Current leaning: B. QT. The strongest argument is that QT has an extra independent straight route: T-9-8-7-6 using the T in hand, needing only 9-8-7-6 on board. QJ’s apparent extra J-T-9-8-7 route is redundant because any board containing T-9-8-7 already gives QJ the higher Q-J-T-9-8 straight via T-9-8 on board. Thus QJ’s independent three-card straight requirements are A-K-T, K-T-9, and T-9-8. QT’s are symmetric A-K-J, K-J-9, and J-9-8, plus the extra 9-8-7-6 low straight. This gives QT strictly more straight boards than QJ (roughly 9,728 additional boards before accounting for hero redraws), so QT should slightly reduce AA equity more than QJ.

Q9 has fewer high/middle three-card straight completions: mainly K-J-T and J-T-8, plus several four-card low-straight requirements, so it likely has the least equity of the three. Colleagues 2 and 5 correctly emphasize QT’s extra T-low straight. Colleagues 3, 4, and 6 favor QJ but appear to overlook that J-T-9-8-7 does not add new QJ straight boards beyond Q-J-T-9-8. Colleague 1’s Q9 argument is flawed in claiming board aces are unavailable: only the two black aces are in hero’s hand, so the red aces remain live. Colleague 7’s “both QJ and QT” is close, but QT appears to have a small extra edge. Pending exact equity confirmation, the best answer is QT.}

